Subject: [PATCH] fix(preview): bound verification in the prompt, and fix the
 two Windows-only test failures
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Run 8 answered the search question (the teaching error fired twice; the agent switched to literal
patterns) and the refusal question (⛔ instantly on `npx serve`, then `start_terminal` — the fix, as the
desktop agent correctly read it). It also repeated run 7's shape: no terminal frame, mid-verify, edits
landed (`M index.html +1 -1`, `M styles.css +1 -1`). Recovery proven twice; completion unreached twice.

**Why it never finishes: nothing bounds verification — and the close invited it.** The prompt ended
"After editing, re-render the preview so the change can be checked in place", which reads as an
instruction to render, i.e. to start something. Run 7 started `npx serve`; run 8 was refused it,
pivoted correctly, and was still polling when the budget ended. The close now says what is enough and
what is not:

    After editing, re-read the lines you changed to confirm them. Do not start a dev server, watcher,
    or any other long-running process to check your work — the preview window re-renders the page
    itself, and the user sees the result in place.

Mirrored byte-for-byte in crusorAIPreview.ts (verified: `node --experimental-strip-types` renders it
and the parity pin compares a distinctive fragment in both), and the old assertion in
test_preview_prompt.py that pinned the previous wording is updated with the evidence for the change.

**The two Windows failures were mine, and both were tests asserting the platform instead of the code:**

* `test_snapshot_reads_content…` wrote a fixture with `write_text`, which translates `\n` to `\r\n` on
  Windows, then asserted LF bytes — green here, red there. Now written and compared as bytes, and it
  also pins that `snapshot()` must NOT normalize line endings (CRLF in, CRLF out).
* `test_demo_mode_never_points_at_the_repository` used `os.path.commonpath`, which RAISES ValueError
  across drives — and TEMP is on C: while the checkout is on D: on the owner's machine. Replaced with
  `is_relative_to`, which answers the same question and returns False instead of raising.

**`--diff` no longer calls a line-ending change a rewrite.** A Windows checkout hands the workspace
CRLF; an agent-written edit may land LF; comparing raw bytes then printed the whole file as
removed-and-re-added, burying the one line that actually changed — the opposite of this tool's job. An
EOL-only difference is now named (`M styles.css   (line endings only — CRLF/LF, no text change)`).
Narrow on purpose: CRLF-normalize both sides and require equality, so a real edit cannot hide. Verified
both ways here without a Windows machine — the CRLF bytes are written directly, and the control that
removes the rule fails the new test while the "real edit next to an EOL difference" case still reports
`+1 -1` with the changed line.

**The budget is now visible.** Run 8 was reported as hitting "the 300 s budget" while the default has
always been 900 (`DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_S`); nothing in the harness or runbook says 300, so the cap came from
the invocation or an assumption. The run prints `turn budget: 900s (default 900s; override with
--timeout)` before it starts, and a timeout now reports how much work had happened
(`the engine had sent N frame(s), M tool call(s), and was still working`) plus the flag that raises it,
so "stuck" and "slow" are told apart instead of guessed.

21 pins in the harness test file, 54 passed across the prompt/contract/harness/terminal-guard lanes.
The 5 failures in the wide sweep are the known sandbox-only set (3 bridge-transport typing_extensions,
2 bundle tests needing a rebuilt tracked `out/`), unchanged from the previous tip.

